---
description: >
  Four drift cycles documented. Cycles 1+2+3 (fixed-`brain`-key drift,
  agent enumeration, post-move identity) are resolved. Cycle 4 —
  post-schema-collapse drift — is active. The schema collapse retired
  backend identity from package code, but this spec still teaches
  "Obsidian today, others tomorrow" / "regardless of which backend
  driver sits under it" / "AIDE is opinionated about the knowledge
  layer: Obsidian today" framings.
intent: >
  After the schema-collapse settlement in `.aide/intent.aide`, the
  package no longer recognizes "backends" at all — backend identity
  inference, the registry, and dispatch on connector identity are
  retired entirely. This spec, stamped aligned in a prior cycle, still
  carries fossilized backend-aware language in its intent paragraph,
  Context, Strategy, and Bad examples. The phrasings are not
  load-bearing for the helper's behavior (the helper installs canonical
  agent files verbatim and the `mcpServers: [brain]` frontmatter rule
  is correct) but they teach a model the root spec retired, which lets
  future reviewers re-litigate decisions the architecture has settled.
misalignment:
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

### Active (alignment pass — post-schema-collapse drift)

- [ ] `src/service/install/installAgents/.aide` intent paragraph ("...declared by name reference as `mcpServers: [brain]`, matching the fixed `brain` MCP key the host project wires under in `.mcp.json`...") combined with outcomes.desired ("regardless of which backend driver sits under it"): the intent and that outcome carry "backend driver" language. Per `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15] (the in-code backend registry and drivers are fully retired) the package no longer has a notion of a "backend driver." The host's brain.aide IS the configuration; there is no driver layer the package recognizes. The MCP key invariant (`mcpServers: [brain]`) is correct; the framing that something is "the driver underneath the key" is the retired model.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15] (registry retired, "Adding a new brain is a user-side edit, never a code-level extension to this package."); `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` outcomes.undesired ("Branching on `name`... re-introduces the identity-inference anti-pattern the brainBackends retirement was meant to delete").
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/installAgents/.aide` Context paragraph ("...the host project wires whichever backend (Obsidian today, others tomorrow) under the fixed `brain` MCP key in `.mcp.json`, and agents reference the brain by that fixed key"): the parenthetical "(Obsidian today, others tomorrow)" is the same backend-aware framing. The user wires WHATEVER they wired — the package never enumerates "which backend" or maintains a roster of valid ones. Suggested rephrase: drop the parenthetical entirely, or replace with "regardless of how the user has configured their brain.aide."
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15] (no enumeration of connectors); outcomes.undesired[5] (no branching on connector identity).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/installAgents/.aide` Strategy ("Agents declare MCP servers by name reference, not inline config. An agent that needs brain access declares `mcpServers: [brain]` — the literal `brain` is the fixed MCP key the host project wires under in `.mcp.json`, regardless of which backend (Obsidian today, others tomorrow) sits in the entry's command/args. This keeps agents portable across vaults *and* across backends: the agent never names a specific backend; the host project picks the backend by what they wire under the `brain` key."): "regardless of which backend" / "across backends" / "picks the backend" all carry the retired backend-resolution frame. The fixed-key invariant is correct; the agnosticism the helper provides is across user configurations, not across packaged backends. (The package has no backends — only a parser that reads whatever the user wrote.)
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15], outcomes.undesired[5]; `.aide/intent.aide` Strategy paragraph "Brain configuration is user-owned, not code-owned."
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/installAgents/.aide` Bad example ("...names a specific backend (e.g., `mcpServers: [obsidian]`) instead of the fixed `brain` key. Both fail the same portability rule — the host project has a different vault and may wire a different backend under the `brain` key. The agent fails on first brain access."): "may wire a different backend" — same framing. The example's structural point (use `[brain]`, not a connector-specific name) is correct; the explanatory frame ("may wire a different backend") teaches the retired model.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15].
  Misalignment: spec-gap

